From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: genuine S+C downloads

While the S-R mode is active, the rtl8_4s switch steers a frame that
arrives from its CPU port with S-tag 3910 + p to port p alone, and
delivers it in the VLAN of its C-tag, tagged or untagged as the port's
membership of that VLAN says. This patch accepts downloads towards the
switch's user ports in that form, instead of declining them as the S-R
mode did until now.

A download towards a user port is classified by the same resolver as an
upload: no VLAN push is the port's untagged class U(p), a push of v is
T(p, v). It is judged in the same two steps (lockless on the published
topology, then under the lock, where the class and its MTU cap must come
out of the topology as it is now as they did from the one judged on),
needs no identity, and must be in WP3's scope for its class and for its
client, the translated destination. Its nexthop leaves through the
conduit's PPE port and the conduit's own L3 interface (a class's L3 device
has the conduit's MAC address, so a flow with any other source MAC is
declined) with an S-tag of 3910 + p and a C-tag of the class's VLAN: the
resolver alone chooses that VLAN, since the switch does not check it
against the port's membership. Its PMTU is capped by the class: the
port's, the bridge's and the VLAN's L3 device's MTU, and the conduit's
less the two tags. Profile E's egress registers are not touched: the arm
required them in place, and the S-R mode owns the egress S-TPID. Under
profile E nothing changes, but that its VLAN-1 check is profile E's only.

The download's class, port, VLAN and form are recorded in its flow entry
before its hardware add, so that the port and scope flushes find it on
every path; the idle verify checks every S-R download against its class
(the conduit's PPE port, the S-tag of its port, which must be in the S-R
port mask, and the C-tag of its VLAN) and finds no profile E download
while the S-R mode is not off; offload_flows shows "sr dn p P form U|T
svid S cvid V nh H", and its "vlan1-tagged" or "vlan1-untagged" only for
profile E's downloads; the status gives each identity's downloads (of
its class) and the downloads accepted.

A client that the bridge learns on another of its ports is followed
(WP2-DESIGN D9): an S-R download names its port in its S-tag, and a
download to a port its client has left is not delivered at all. The FDB
notifier now looks at a learn on any port of the download's bridge, a
Wi-Fi port included, in the download's own VLAN (VLAN 1 being no
different), with the S-R generation of the moment; profile E keeps its
filter (a DSA user port, VLAN 1 or none), and a learn on any other port
is not looked at while the S-R mode is off. The move work lets the
address queue another move first, then takes the download to the port
the bridge has its client on as it runs (br_fdb_find_port()), not to the
one the learn that queued it named: a second learn while it waited
queued nothing. It decides under RTNL and the lock (ppe_sr_eng_move(),
pure): the download is re-pointed, with a new nexthop carrying the new
port's S-tag and the same C-tag, taken before the flow is touched, only
while the S-R mode is active, the generation has not moved since the
learn and neither port awaits a resync, to a user port of the S-R switch
in the S-R port mask that forwards, whose class in that VLAN has the
download's form both in the topology and in the bridge as it is now, in
scope for the client, with an MTU cap no smaller than the download's
PMTU. Anything else drops the download: a port that does not qualify, a
bridge that no longer has the address, a nexthop not to be had. The
flowtable then offers it again with the route it was created with,
towards the port its client was first resolved on, where its software
fast path sends it too. The moved client's uploads are left alone (they
come with another identity and fail the source check). The flap limit
and the cap on moves in flight are profile E's; an S-R download whose
learn finds no room for a move, or no memory for one, is marked for a
sweep on the same workqueue, which takes it to its client as its move
would have.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
